Your MCP server can now send[events](https://mastra.ai/docs/connections/mcp#mcp-events) to connected clients using push, poll, or webhook delivery methods.


Agents and apps often need to act on changes that occur outside of conversations, like a failed deploy, merged pull request, new signup, or change in order status. MCP events give both ends of the pipe a standard way to` .emit()` and` .subscribe()` to events without the need for an additional pub/sub service.


Your browser does not support the video tag.


Before MCP events, MCP servers could only respond to requests. If connected clients needed updates, they'd need to poll the server, which often required additional tooling and added complexity. Now, building on Mastra's[MCP 2.0](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-mcp-2) support, events can be used to make two-way communication easier to manage.


When[observability](https://mastra.ai/docs/observability/overview) is configured, MCP event traces are captured automatically.


## Get started


In this example, GitHub sends a request to a[webhook](https://docs.github.com/en/webhooks/about-webhooks) URL whenever repo activity occurs. The webhook URL is a[custom Mastra API endpoint](https://mastra.ai/docs/server/custom-api-routes) , which is used to emit events from a Mastra` MCPServer` . A desktop Electron app subscribes to the same events using the Mastra` MCPClient` .


Terminal


```text
npm   install   @mastra/mcp@latest   @mastra/core@latest
```


note


Requires` @mastra/mcp@2.5.0` and` @mastra/core@1.79.0` or later, added in[PR #27324](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/27324) .


Define events on the MCP server using` defineEvent()` :


src/mastra/mcp/server.ts


```text
export   const   github   =   new   MCPServer  ({
// ...
events: {
"  repo.starred  "  :   defineEvent  ({
description:   "  Someone starred the repository  "  ,
inputSchema: z.  object  ({ repo: z.  string  () }),
payloadSchema: z.  object  ({
repo: z.  string  (),
user: z.  string  (),
stars: z.  number  ()
}),
match  : (args, data)   =>   {
return   data.repo   ===   args.repo;
}
})
}
});
```


Publish events using` .emit()` , passing in the data from the event payload:


src/mastra/index.ts


```text
export   const   mastra   =   new   Mastra  ({
mcpServers: { github },
server: {
apiRoutes: [
registerApiRoute  (  "  /github/webhook  "  , {
method:   "  POST  "  ,
handler  :   async   (context)   =>   {
const   event   =   context.req.  header  (  "  X-GitHub-Event  "  );
const   body   =   await   context.req.  json  ();


if   (event   ===   "  star  "   &&   body.action   ===   "  created  "  ) {
await   github.events.  emit  (  "  repo.starred  "  , {
repo: body.repository.full_name,
user: body.sender.login,
stars: body.repository.stargazers_count
});
}


return   context.  json  ({ ok:   true   });
}
})
]
}
});
```


Subscribe to events using` .subscribe()` to handle each event as it arrives. The` .subscribe()` method takes up to four arguments:


- ` "github"` : The name of the MCP server in the client's` servers` config.
- ` "repo.starred"` : The name of the event to subscribe to.
- ` { repo }` : The arguments defined by the event's` inputSchema` .
- ` { delivery }` : Optional. How events are delivered to the subscription.


src/octocat-app/main.js


```text
const   mcp   =   new   MCPClient  ({
servers: {
github: {
url:   new   URL  (  "  http://<mcp-server-url>/api/mcp/github/mcp  "  )
}
}
});


const   starSubscription   =   await   mcp.events.  subscribe  (  "  github  "  ,   "  repo.starred  "  ,   {   repo:   "  PaulieScanlon/mastra-mcp-events  "   },   {   delivery:   "  push  "   });


for   await   (  const   event   of   starSubscription)   {
const   {   user,   stars   }   =   event.data;
// trigger star animation with user and star count
}
```


## Delivery methods


Set` delivery` on` .subscribe()` to define the subscription behavior. The default is` push` :


- ` push` : The subscription holds one` events/stream` request open listening for server events.
- ` poll` : The subscription repeatedly asks the server for new events, at most once a second by default.
- ` webhook` : The server sends each event to a URL on the client as a signed POST request. Requires` webhook` settings on the` MCPClient` .


## Events and agents


Agents can react to MCP events using[MCPEventsSignalProvider](https://mastra.ai/reference/tools/mcp-client#events-in-agents-and-workflows-experimental) . Each event is delivered to the agent's thread as a notification:


```text
const   mcp   =   new   MCPClient  ({
// ...
});


const   starSignal   =   new   MCPEventsSignalProvider  ({
client: mcp,
subscribeOptions: { delivery:   "  push  "   }
});


export   const   repoAgent   =   new   Agent  ({
// ...
signals: [starSignal]
});


await   starSignal.  subscribeThread  (
{   resourceId:   "  repo-agent  "  ,   threadId:   "  thread-1  "   },
{
server:   "  github  "  ,
event:   "  repo.starred  "  ,
args: { repo:   "  PaulieScanlon/mastra-mcp-events  "   }
}
);
```


## Events and workflows


Workflows can react to MCP events using a[subscription](https://mastra.ai/reference/tools/mcp-client#events-in-agents-and-workflows-experimental) . Each event can be used to start a workflow run:


```text
import   {   repoWorkflow   }   from   "  ./src/mastra/workflows/repo-workflow.ts  "  ;


const   mcp   =   new   MCPClient  ({
// ...
});


for   await   (  const   event   of   starSubscription)   {
const   run   =   await   repoWorkflow.  createRun  ();
await   run.  start  ({   inputData: event.data });
}
```


For more information and full configuration options, see:


- [MCP events](https://mastra.ai/docs/connections/mcp#mcp-events)
- [MCPServer events reference](https://mastra.ai/reference/tools/mcp-server#events-experimental)
- [MCPClient events reference](https://mastra.ai/reference/tools/mcp-client#events-property-experimental)
- [MCPEventsSignalProvider reference](https://mastra.ai/reference/tools/mcp-client#events-in-agents-and-workflows-experimental)
- [Custom API routes](https://mastra.ai/docs/server/custom-api-routes)
- [About webhooks](https://docs.github.com/en/webhooks/about-webhooks)


MCP events are still experimental — the API may change between releases.
